{"step_index":840,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-27T01:58:19Z","thinking":"**Analyzing Visual Features**\n\nOkay, I'm examining the SVG file now. I've noted the `viewBox` dimensions and am focusing on Bé Bin's detailed facial features. The ahoge, the eyebrows' softness, anime eyes with dual highlights, and rosy blushing are all interesting visual aspects. I'm taking note of the level of detail.\n\n\n**Comparing Visual Elements**\n\nI've shifted my attention to `intro_act1_wardrobe.svg` and discovered a jarring discrepancy. The file contains a simplistic stick-figure design, contrasting sharply with the detailed Bé Bin character in previous files. The user's \"image error\" feedback now makes perfect sense - the wrong character was displayed. The wardrobe's crude representation is the culprit.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Root Causes**\n\nI've traced the image error back to the `index.html` file and the `ensureBinRenderer()` function. The function uses a hardcoded list of SVG assets for Bé Bin's base representation. When outfits are layered, `setOutfitLayer()` incorrectly positions miniature outfit icons over the base character. The `shirt_soft.svg`, `pants_soft.svg` and `shoes_velcro.svg` icons are not properly scaled or positioned as outfit layers. The issue lies in the icon being pasted onto the rendered image without the intended image dimensions, causing them to appear tiny. The root issue is both the base assets and how the outfits are rendered.\n\n\n**Integrating Character Data**\n\nI'm now integrating the locked Bé Bin character data into `intro_act1_wardrobe.svg`. I'm going through the details, paying close attention to the `viewBox`, proportions, and all the features, especially the watercolor blushing. The goal is to represent Bé Bin in a thoughtful pose, touching his chin and contemplating an outfit. The scene will be in his bedroom.\n\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"<svg xmlns=\